WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:50.519
The end of 2018 couldn’t have been

00:00:50.519 --> 00:01:16.850
more stressful: the 
protests of the so-called yellow vests have

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:27.720
unleashed a wave of violence and political
rebellion in Emmanuel Macron’s France.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:32.670
Increasing fuel taxes to reduce its consumption
[and, also, to increase income] has infuriated

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.360
a large part of French society.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:39.240
So, to reject this measure [And to take advantage
and ask for more changes] hundreds of thousands

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:42.680
of people mobilized and took to the streets
wearing yellow vests.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:48.620
I think we all know what came next: thousands
of arrests, hundreds wounded and a government

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:55.530
held against the ropes, forced to take a step
back.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:02:06.280
But, just a second, because in this video
we’re going to talk about China, not France.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.379
Then why the hell are we talking about the
yellow vests?

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:17.800
No, folks, I haven’t lost my mind.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:22.980
France’s incident may have led to a more
violent result than expected, but it isn’t

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.730
an unusual one.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:28.439
When a society doesn’t feel represented
by its politicians or believes that their

00:02:28.439 --> 00:02:33.129
rights as citizens are being violated, protests
and demonstrations are commonplace.

00:02:33.129 --> 00:02:35.680
However, this doesn’t happen in China.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.730
Which leads to a few questions:

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.049
Why do we never see protests like this in
China?

00:02:41.049 --> 00:02:44.379
Don’t the Chinese have more than enough
reasons to feel dissatisfied?

00:02:44.379 --> 00:02:49.459
And perhaps the key question, how does the
Chinese government manage to keep 1.3 billion

00:02:49.459 --> 00:02:52.209
people under control and all without any sign
of discontent?

00:02:52.209 --> 00:02:55.849
Okay, some of you may be thinking, “obviously,
it's a dictatorship.”

00:02:55.849 --> 00:03:02.450
Yes, you’re right, but that doesn’t tell
the whole story.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:05.920
As you know, there’s always more to it.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.599
Listen up.

00:03:09.599 --> 00:03:12.489
(THE CHINESE NON-CHANGE)

00:03:12.489 --> 00:03:19.629
Despite the reforms, of which we’ve already
spoken here on VisualPolitik, 20 or 30 years

00:03:19.629 --> 00:03:26.209
ago it was expected that today’s China would
be very different.

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:30.420
Many experts predicted that as the Chinese
economy began to prosper and its middle class

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:35.260
to grow, within a short time social demands
would cause a change in the political system

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:39.370
towards a more open, free, transparent and
less corrupt system.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:43.239
Since then, the country has experienced an
impressive economic takeoff and the Chinese

00:03:43.239 --> 00:03:49.480
middle class is now composed of hundreds of
millions of people...

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:53.950
But still there’s no trace of riots.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:59.560
Ok, there have been protests, but there’s
no comparison... and for many Chinese people,

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:06.150
seeing a demonstration is like seeing someone
drinking Carling beer: you know it happens,

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:10.310
but it’s weird to see it for yourself.

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:14.430
And not only that, but with Xi Jinping in
power the exact opposite is happening.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:19.250
While Chinese society is experiencing its
best moment in history in economic terms,

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.970
Xi Jinping is the most powerful president
since Mao.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:28.560
In fact he won’t even respect the two-mandate
limitation that Chinese leaders had to adhere

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:29.560
to.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.470
Yup, another case like that of Evo Morales
in Bolivia

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:43.009
But... let's go back to the question at hand,

00:04:43.009 --> 00:04:46.949
Why does the Chinese government have so much
control over everything that happens?

00:04:46.949 --> 00:04:51.740
Why hasn’t this control suffered any erosion
despite the fact that now the country is much

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:55.360
richer and much more open to the world?

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:56.720
What can we expect in the future?

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.670
Folks, these are the 3 key questions we’re
going to answer in this video.

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:05.789
And yes, we’ll talk about the repression
machine later... but we can also find some

00:05:05.789 --> 00:05:07.750
additional elements.

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:10.800
Check it out.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.849
(THE PACT)

00:05:13.849 --> 00:05:21.479
A recent New York Times article talked about
how the Chinese government has managed to

00:05:21.479 --> 00:05:28.750
turn their economic development into a way
to legitimize the regime.

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:33.169
And that has a lot to do with a kind of tacit
pact that exists between the Chinese government

00:05:33.169 --> 00:05:35.680
and a good portion of Chinese society.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.699
An agreement based on the following:

00:05:37.699 --> 00:05:41.600
the government promises to do what it can
to achieve better living conditions, and in

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.320
return society, or at least a good part of
it, doesn’t question the government itself

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:49.389
and looks the other way on political issues.

00:05:49.389 --> 00:05:55.250
Here’s a question-- controversial but very
topical: would you be willing to renounce

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:59.970
civil liberties and political participation
in exchange for an improvement in your standard

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:00.970
of living?

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:08.379
Leave your answer in the comments and also
in this survey.

00:06:08.379 --> 00:06:13.940
But, just a second.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:17.840
This is all very easy to say... but not as
easy to carry out.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:21.031
So the question we can ask ourselves is, how
on earth has the Chinese government managed

00:06:21.031 --> 00:06:24.430
to fulfill its part of the bargain?

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:29.060
Well, we’ve already talked about one of
the issues on VisualPolitik, in a video we

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:32.960
made about the reforms this country carried
out after Mao’s death.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:37.600
To create wealth, the Chinese government put
aside economic Marxism and freed the forces

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:41.520
of private enterprise, competition, globalization
and capitalism.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.349
Yes, we’ve already told you this, but...
that’s not all.

00:06:46.349 --> 00:06:50.730
The other element that the government has
used has been none other than education.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:53.800
And not only because they control the content,
which we all know.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:58.270
See, to make everyone feel like they had a
chance, especially the almost 40% of the rural

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:02.479
population that’s still mostly poor – and
this amounts to more than 500 million people

00:07:02.479 --> 00:07:06.130
in the part of China that doesn’t have Shanghai’s
skyscrapers – the Chinese government has

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:10.860
created a social narrative by which anyone
who strives, regardless of their economic

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:16.250
level, can climb the ladder of social mobility
and reach the highest echelons.

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:20.409
How did they do that?

00:07:20.409 --> 00:07:25.710
Well, through universities and an access system
known as Gaokao.

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:30.150
The Gaokao is a very hard test that, if you
pass it, guarantees the opportunity to study

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:35.240
at a higher education institution regardless
of your income level or your family's situation.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:40.070
Because in China, unless you have money, access
isn’t universal, far from it.

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:45.660
Each year 9 million young people compete for
only tens of thousands of places.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:50.620
And most young people know that University
can change their life.

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:57.939
You can already imagine the incredible amount
of competition that exists to pass this exam.

00:07:57.939 --> 00:08:02.840
But in a country where corruption in institutions
is usually part of the game, Gaokao is seen

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:09.310
as an opportunity, a relatively fair test
that any student can access.

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:13.550
That way, the narrative that the Chinese government
has built for years is that in this country

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:17.430
there’s full social mobility, any young
person regardless of their family’s income

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:20.639
level can progress up the social scale.

00:08:20.639 --> 00:08:25.479
And if they don’t achieve that progress,
it’s their fault.

00:08:25.479 --> 00:08:29.599
(AUDIO: “Gaokao allows the government to
say: ‘If you are not successful, you can

00:08:29.599 --> 00:08:30.819
only blame yourself.

00:08:30.819 --> 00:08:34.200
You did not work hard enough [...] That is
a very powerful way of governing.”

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:38.200
- Yong Zhao, an education professor at the
University of Kansas.)

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:42.380
Actually it really isn’t like that, the
Gaokao is a system that favors some skills

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:45.190
over others, and therefore some students over
others.

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:49.560
But the Chinese government has managed to
introduce this interpretation of equality

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:53.209
into society.

00:08:53.209 --> 00:09:03.000
That’s how the Chinese government has kept
Chinese society relatively satisfied over

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:08.100
the past few years.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:12.260
The Chinese have seen how their lives improve
year after year and thanks to the Gaokao and

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:16.630
a university system that allows for social
mobility, everyone feels like opportunities

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:17.980
are available to everyone.

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:22.070
In addition, the Gaokao favors a study model
based on memory and repetition and not on

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:24.130
creativity and critical analysis.

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:26.310
But we’ll talk about that some other time.

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:27.889
But that’s not all.

00:09:27.889 --> 00:09:33.000
Let's continue.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:43.070
(TIME PERSPECTIVE)

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:46.320
The second question that might explain why
the country’s economic growth hasn’t been

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:49.900
accompanied by political demands has a lot
to do with the speed of the changes.

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:55.450
Allow me to explain, social and cultural changes
often happen much more slowly than economic

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:59.920
and technological changes.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:07.100
Yes, China has experienced a huge amount of
development but not enough time has passed

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:09.120
for political demands to emerge.

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:10.120
This isn’t strange.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:15.190
If we take a look at the history of Western
democracies, both in Europe and even in America,

00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:19.730
we can see that political changes have always
been much slower than economic changes.

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:21.930
Although they did arrive eventually.

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:27.130
But we can’t forget that countries such
as Spain, Portugal, Taiwan, South Korea and

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:30.880
the nations of Eastern Europe, among many
others, suffered under dictatorships of all

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:33.680
kinds until only a few decades ago.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:39.420
In other words, the Chinese are still moving
from poverty to a more comfortable lifestyle.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:43.230
But yes, it’s obvious that we can’t talk
about the Chinese government’s control over

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:47.310
its citizens without discussing coercion.

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:51.389
Listen up.

00:10:51.389 --> 00:10:56.589
(THE POWER OF THE FORCE)

00:10:56.589 --> 00:11:00.420
I think none of us will be surprised if I
say that the Chinese government has more than

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:01.850
enough tools to subdue protests.

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:07.279
At the end of the day China is still a dictatorship
that leads in rankings like these:

00:11:07.279 --> 00:11:12.600
(AUDIO: “China named 'world’s top executioner'
as global rate falls.

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:16.630
Amnesty says more people executed in China
than rest of world combined.”

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:17.630
- The Guardian)

00:11:17.630 --> 00:11:20.649
(AUDIO: "China is ‘the world’s biggest
prison for journalists".

00:11:20.649 --> 00:11:21.649
- Reporters Without Borders)

00:11:21.649 --> 00:11:25.310
Folks, the Chinese government’s control
is so great that as you all know they’ve

00:11:25.310 --> 00:11:29.139
basically been able to create their own Internet.

00:11:29.139 --> 00:11:33.639
In China, this network is so censored that
services like Google, YouTube and Facebook

00:11:33.639 --> 00:11:36.550
as well as millions of web pages are blocked.

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:40.790
Except for the use of VPN networks – which
are of course illegal – the Chinese only

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:44.060
see the information that the Chinese government
allows them to see.

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:45.820
And there’s more.

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:49.690
It seems that by 2020 the Chinese government
wants to implement a kind of ranking that

00:11:49.690 --> 00:11:55.750
will classify each and every Chinese citizen
based on a lot of variables: credit, financial,

00:11:55.750 --> 00:11:58.420
social, political, legal, etc., etc.

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:03.160
That is, the Chinese government wants to create
a ranking of good and bad citizens.

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:05.589
Have you seen Black Mirror?

00:12:05.589 --> 00:12:10.759
(AUDIO: “Black Mirror is almost a reality:
China says it's on track to launch its dystopian

00:12:10.759 --> 00:12:15.331
scorecard system that ranks citizens on every
aspect of their behaviour by 2020”.

00:12:15.331 --> 00:12:16.331
- Daily Mirror)

00:12:16.331 --> 00:12:17.331
(AUDIO: “It will affect eligibility for
loans, jobs, even where your children go to

00:12:17.331 --> 00:12:18.331
school [...] For example, citizens with low
scores will not be hired by certain employers

00:12:18.331 --> 00:12:19.331
and will be forbidden from obtaining some
jobs, including in the civil service, journalism

00:12:19.331 --> 00:12:20.331
and legal fields, where of course you must
be deemed trustworthy”.

00:12:20.331 --> 00:12:21.331
- Rachel Botsman, author of Who Can You Trust?)

00:12:21.331 --> 00:12:25.240
Obviously, political issues will carry a great
deal of weight, so being critical of the Beijing

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:29.670
government could place you among the most
unreliable citizens, which would close most

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:33.910
doors, both personal and professional, for
you.

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:35.870
Terrifying, right?

00:12:35.870 --> 00:12:40.830
Well, now you can see the tools that the Chinese
government employs to maintain its control

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:41.830
over society.

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:46.040
First, The political pact and the promise
of prosperity and opportunity for all; Second,

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:50.449
The fact that social change moves more slowly
than economic change; and Third, the huge

00:12:50.449 --> 00:12:55.519
amount of control and repression that the
Chinese government uses are all reasons why

00:12:55.519 --> 00:12:59.339
there are no yellow vests in China.

00:12:59.339 --> 00:13:05.510
But the question we can all ask ourselves
is, will the Chinese government perpetuate

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:11.360
its model or is this country going to suffer
a wave of democratic change and openness?

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:12.550
Leave your answer in the comments.

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:16.529
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did, and don’t forget to

00:13:16.529 --> 00:13:18.180
subscribe for brand new videos.

